Abstract

Standard nucleosynthesis models impose the Markovian master equation as an external hypothesis, with transition rates relying on phenomenological reaction cross-sections and artificial mass truncations. Based solely on the combinatorial postulates of nodes and directed shared links, this paper proves that the dynamics of nuclide abundances are discrete counting identities of shared-link reconfiguration, requiring no statistical add-ons whatsoever. We construct the third-order transition tensor for checker-jump aggregation and the topological fragmentation operator; all operator elements are combinatorially generated from defect-node counts, the hierarchical truncation criterion, and the algebraic sum of topological charge directions, without any adjustable parameters. The steady-state abundance expression is derived algebraically, the three-factor net retention criterion is recovered, and the peak abundance of the iron peak is given a purely topological explanation based on geometric extrema of inflow and outflow. The correspondence between eigenmodes under linear perturbation and stellar timescales is presented only as a working hypothesis, with the complete spectral analysis relegated to the open problems. Three qualitative, falsifiable astronomical predictions that rely only on the proven geometric constructions are proposed, and an independent section comprehensively lists all unclosed mathematical boundaries, strictly distinguishing proven combinatorial conclusions from analytic derivations yet to be completed. A first-principles topological foundation for nucleosynthesis, free of exogenous hypotheses, is thereby established.

