§2. Combinatorial Postulates and Topological Nuclide Encoding

All conclusions in this paper are built upon the following three fundamental postulates, without introducing any additional physical quantities or continuous geometric background.

Postulate 1 (Combinatorial Substrate): There exists a discrete combinatorial network, consisting of nodes and directed edges connecting the nodes. This network is not embedded in any pre-existing space and does not evolve in any pre-existing time. The only primitive relation between nodes and edges is “connected” or “not connected.” The only primitive attribute of an edge is its direction—pointing from one node to another.

Postulate 2 (Local Reconnection and Direction Conservation): The sole dynamics of the substrate is local reconnection—when two edges meet at a node, they may disconnect and reconnect according to directional rules. Directional rule: when traversing any closed loop, the difference between the number of counterclockwise-directed edges and clockwise-directed edges remains invariant.

