§7.1 Ontological Comparison with the Standard Phenomenological Nucleosynthesis Framework

Existing REACLIB-driven s-/r-process models possess three layers of irreducible exogenous hypotheses: (1) pre-implanted Markovian stochastic dynamics; (2) experimentally fitted reaction cross-sections as transition rates; (3) artificially imposed mass-neighbor truncations to compress the matrix dimension.

This framework achieves a three-layer bottom-level replacement:

Markovian dynamics emerge from shared-link counting identities, without pre-assuming any stochastic process;
All transition weights are replaced by pure counts of topological defect nodes, without experimentally fitted cross-sections;
The hierarchical truncation criterion is automatically generated by the geometry of the recursive growth tree; cross-mass checker-jump channels are naturally allowed, requiring no manual relaxation of matrix restrictions.
The observational distinguishability between the two is fully covered by the three qualitative predictions in §7; future high-precision spectroscopy and gravitational-wave observations can directly judge the validity of the two frameworks.

